[33.842681, "o", "\u001b[37m```\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m## What this gives you\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m| Requirement | How it's satisfied |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m| Group-owned by `research` | `chown root:research` |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m| setgid bit | mode `2770` + `# flags: -s-` (new files/dirs inherit group `research`) |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m| Base perms 2770 | `drwxrws---` |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m| research rwx (access + default) | `group:research:rwx`, `default:group:research:rwx` |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m| alice & bob rwx (access + default) | `user:alice:rwx`, `user:bob:rwx` + defaults |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m| Others none (access + default) | `other::---`, `default:other::---` |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m| Proper mask | `mask::rwx`, `default:mask::rwx` |\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m## Verification (done in a mirrored throwaway dir, then deleted — `/srv/shared` left empty)\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m- alice created a file with `umask 077` → result `-rw-rw---- alice:research`, ACL inherited with `bob:rwx` effective `rw-`. ACL overrides the umask, so inheritance is reliable.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m- Subdir created by alice → `drwxrws--- 2770 alice:research` with the full default ACL propagated, so inheritance continues arbitrarily deep.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m- bob successfully **read and appended** to alice's file → cross-user access works both ways for alice/bob.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m- `nobody` (outside `research`) → `Permission denied` on both listing and reading, enforced by `other::---` on current and default ACLs plus the `2770` mode.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m`/srv/shared` contains 0 entries, so the tests can create their own files. Note that newly created *files* will show `rw-` rather than `rwx` for the named users — that's normal POSIX behavior (the `x` bit isn't inherited into regular files), while directories get the full `rwx`; read/write ACL access for alice and bob is unaffected.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
